{"name":"Venous Blood Red","hex":"#3f3033","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"venous","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to veins.","Having passed through the capillaries and given up oxygen for the tissues and become charged with carbon dioxide."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/venous"},{"term":"blood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vital liquid flowing in the bodies of many types of animals that usually conveys nutrients and oxygen. In vertebrates, it is colored red by hemoglobin, is conveyed by arteries and veins, is pumped by the heart and is usually generated in bone marrow.","A family relationship due to birth, such as that between siblings; contrasted with relationships due to marriage or adoption. See blood relative, blood relation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause something to be covered with blood; to bloody.","To let blood (from); to bleed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blood"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blood red","blocks":[{"text":"The colour blood red is a dark shade of the colour red meant to resemble the colour of human blood. It is the iron in hemoglobin specifically that gives blood its red colour. The actual colour ranges from crimson to a dark brown-blood depending on how oxygenated the blood is, and may have a slightly orange hue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Blood_Test_%2815575812743%29.jpg/330px-Blood_Test_%2815575812743%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood_red","wikidata":"Q48988258"},{"title":"Venous blood","blocks":[{"text":"Venous blood is deoxygenated blood which travels from the peripheral blood vessels, through the venous system into the right atrium of the heart. Deoxygenated blood is then pumped by the right ventricle to the lungs via the pulmonary artery which is divided in two branches, left and right to the left and right lungs respectively. Blood is oxygenated in the lungs and returns to the left atrium through the pulmonary veins."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Bloodbags.jpg/330px-Bloodbags.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venous_blood","wikidata":"Q618363"},{"title":"Vein","blocks":[{"text":"A vein is a blood vessel in the circulatory system of humans and most other animals that carries blood towards the heart. Most veins carry deoxygenated blood from the tissues back to the heart; exceptions are those of the pulmonary and fetal circulations which carry oxygenated blood to the heart. In the systemic circulation, arteries carry oxygenated blood away from the heart, and veins return deoxygenated blood to the heart, in the deep veins."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Vein_%28retouched%29.svg/330px-Vein_%28retouched%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vein","wikidata":"Q9609"},{"title":"Blood","blocks":[{"text":"Blood is a specialized form of connective tissue and body fluid in the circulatory system of humans and other vertebrates that delivers necessary substances such as nutrients and oxygen to the cells of the body, and transports metabolic waste products away from those same cells."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Venous_and_arterial_blood.jpg/330px-Venous_and_arterial_blood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood","wikidata":"Q7873"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Venous_and_arterial_blood_rotated_and_cropped.jpg/500px-Venous_and_arterial_blood_rotated_and_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Venous_and_arterial_blood_rotated_and_cropped.jpg/1280px-Venous_and_arterial_blood_rotated_and_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Venous_and_arterial_blood_rotated_and_cropped.jpg/500px-Venous_and_arterial_blood_rotated_and_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Difference in hue between venous (darker/blackish red) blood and arterial (brighter/carmine red) blood","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVenous_and_arterial_blood_rotated_and_cropped.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Figure_40_03_07.jpg/500px-Figure_40_03_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Figure_40_03_07.jpg/500px-Figure_40_03_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Layers of vein wall shown in comparison to arterial wall","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFigure_40_03_07.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/2131_Major_Systematic_Veins.jpg/500px-2131_Major_Systematic_Veins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/2131_Major_Systematic_Veins.jpg/1280px-2131_Major_Systematic_Veins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/2131_Major_Systematic_Veins.jpg/500px-2131_Major_Systematic_Veins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2131_Major_Systematic_Veins.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/2134_Thoracic_Upper_Limb_Veins.jpg/500px-2134_Thoracic_Upper_Limb_Veins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/2134_Thoracic_Upper_Limb_Veins.jpg/1280px-2134_Thoracic_Upper_Limb_Veins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/2134_Thoracic_Upper_Limb_Veins.jpg/500px-2134_Thoracic_Upper_Limb_Veins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Deep and superficial veins of the arm and near thorax","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2134_Thoracic_Upper_Limb_Veins.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Venous_valve_00013.gif/500px-Venous_valve_00013.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Venous_valve_00013.gif/500px-Venous_valve_00013.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Video of a valve in the popliteal vein opening to allow blood to flow through and closing to prevent backflow","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVenous_valve_00013.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg/500px-2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/